59,3,,status,Asuka,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option A is the most logical choice. It covers our fixed overheads immediately and removes the transactional friction that @Shunichi noted causes people to withdraw. Most importantly, it avoids the scrutiny of who is attending what.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"I must wholeheartedly agree with Asuka. Option A is the only choice that respects the treasurer's time. As the logs showed, chasing individual payments is a nightmare; a single annual collection is a mercy.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Jonathan,,"While Option A is simple, it ignores the imbalance I found. A flat fee means those who do all the work and attend every meeting effectively subsidize those who barely appear. That isn't fair.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, your obsession with ""fairness"" is becoming a liability. Option A removes the mental hurdle of ""is this worth the cost?"" every single week. If we want people back, we make attendance automatic, not a calculation.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, fairness doesn't matter if the bank account is empty. Option A is the only one that guarantees we cover those 70% fixed costs upfront. We can't gamble on activity-based fees when the rent is due.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, the ""subsidy"" you mention is actually the price of a welcoming environment. If we move to Option B or C, we create a hierarchy of membership that makes newcomers feel like outsiders until they ""level up.""","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Jonathan, your pursuit of equity is creating a logistical labyrinth. Option B requires tracking tiers, and Option C is the very nightmare the previous treasurer spent six hours a month fighting. Logic suggests we choose the path of least resistance.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Benjamin, the path of least resistance often leads to resentment. If the core members feel exploited, they will eventually stop volunteering. We need a system that acknowledges their commitment, perhaps through the tiers in Option B.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, you're imagining a rebellion that isn't happening. The real risk is a club of ghosts. Option B just adds more labels and more decisions. Let's stop overthinking and just make it easy to show up.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, the ""core"" members will be even more resentful if the club folds because we tried to be ""fair"" with a complex system. Option A is the only way to ensure the bills are paid.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, a system that labels members by their ""level"" of commitment is a system that monitors them. We must protect the privacy of our members' contributions and attendance to keep this place a sanctuary, not a ledger.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Jonathan, you are prioritizing a theoretical resentment over a documented burnout. We have evidence that session-based chasing is a grueling task. Why would we intentionally choose a system that guarantees the next treasurer's misery for the sake of ""equity""?","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I am not prioritizing theory, but the reality of our rota. If the core nine people burn out because they feel taken for granted, the club fails regardless of the bank balance. Option B could offer a sustainable compromise.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, you're treating a social club like a labor union. The ""core"" stays because they enjoy it, not because they're auditing their ROI. Option A is the only way to stop the hesitation and bring the atmosphere back.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, the core members are the ones who will suffer most if we can't pay the rent. Option A is the only way to stop the guesswork and secure the money we actually need. I'm voting for A.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Asuka,,Option A is the only choice that preserves the club's inclusive spirit while ensuring financial survival. It removes the barriers for newcomers and the burden on the treasurer. I vote for Option A.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"The evidence is overwhelming. Option C is a recipe for burnout, and Option B is an unnecessary complication. For the sake of the treasurer's sanity and the club's stability, I vote for Option A.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I cannot ignore the imbalance of effort and attendance. While I value simplicity, a system that ignores the core members' contributions is unsustainable. For the sake of long-term fairness, I vote for Option B.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, your ""fairness"" is just a fancy word for a barrier. We need to stop the calculations and start the habit of attending. For the sake of the atmosphere, I vote for Option A.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Takumi,,"I've said it all. We can't run a club on ""fairness"" or ""atmosphere"" if we can't pay the bills. Option A is the only realistic way to secure our fixed costs. I vote for Option A.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
